window.COUNCIL_SPEECHES = {"meta": {"title": "Crossville City Council: 3-minute speech scripts", "count": 22, "perspectives": [{"id": "combined", "label": "Open streams, misuse, and CEO (combined case)", "short": "Combined case"}, {"id": "efficacy", "label": "Money vs. low hit rate", "short": "Hit rate / cost"}, {"id": "security", "label": "Cameras left unsecured (technologist Benn Jordan)", "short": "Unsecured cameras"}, {"id": "abuse", "label": "Abuse cases in Tennessee and Georgia", "short": "TN & GA abuse"}, {"id": "ceo", "label": "CEO & company deception", "short": "CEO / deception"}, {"id": "transparency", "label": "No public transparency / trust-me model", "short": "Transparency"}, {"id": "cancellations", "label": "Cities cancelling and removing Flock", "short": "Cancellations"}, {"id": "liability", "label": "Taxpayer liability from plate misreads", "short": "Liability / lawsuits"}, {"id": "sharing", "label": "Cross-county sharing, profiles & convoys", "short": "Sharing / convoys"}, {"id": "unpopularity", "label": "Unpopular with voters", "short": "Voter unpopularity"}, {"id": "vendor-swap", "label": "Vendor swap is not privacy (Not just Flock)", "short": "Not just Flock"}], "shared_ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "notes": "Replace [Your Name]. Speeches are ready-to-read drafts for public comment. Prefer primary sources on CrossvillePrivacy.org over secondary summaries. Open-stream scripts include speaker-prep stills in the visuals field; do not read those links aloud."}, "speeches": [{"id": "combined-a", "perspective": "combined", "variant": "A", "title": "Open streams, misuse, and a CEO who attacks oversight", "focus_note": "Benn Jordan & Jason Koebler self-record / Milwaukee & Oak Ridge / Staunton Chief Williams", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "I support public safety. I do not support leasing citywide surveillance from a company that has failed on security, enabled abuse, and attacked citizens who ask questions.\n\nFirst: the cameras themselves. In late 2025, technologist Benn Jordan found Flock Condor cameras live-streaming on the open internet. No username. No password. Anyone with a link could watch live video, download about thirty days of archive, and change settings. Security researcher Jon Gaines expanded the find. 404 Media verified roughly sixty exposed cameras. Reporter Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median at Harris Road and Young Street in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself livestream while colleagues watched remotely. Jordan filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that auto-tracked him. Local TV, including Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX, confirmed exposed police camera feeds. Flock called it a \"limited misconfiguration.\" That is a product-security failure, not an activist rumor. If this vendor left live police cameras on the public internet, \"trust our cloud\" is not a policy Crossville should keep buying.\n\nSecond: misuse is not rare. It is a pattern. In Milwaukee, WI, an officer searched a woman he was dating and her ex nearly one hundred eighty times, then resigned and pleaded guilty. One of the detectives who investigated him was later charged for misusing the same system. Georgia newsrooms this summer covered a wave of firings and arrests. Savannah put six employees on leave over alleged personal searches. Closer to home, Oak Ridge's Zachary Gauthier ran personal Flock searches, pleaded guilty to official misconduct, and was sentenced. Cities also face lawsuits in Huntington, WV, Wichita, KS, and California. Taxpayers keep the downside. Flock keeps the lease check.\n\nThird: look at how this company's CEO treats the public. In December 2025, Flock CEO Garrett Langley emailed police clients that they were under a \"coordinated attack\" from activists who want to \"defund the police... and normalize lawlessness,\" and that people were \"trying to turn a public records process into a weapon.\" In a Forbes interview, he also called people who map publicly visible cameras a \"terroristic organization,\" closer to Antifa than to lawful advocacy. Staunton, VA, Police Chief Jim Williams, a forty-one year police veteran, answered him straight. He said residents raising concerns were exercising their rights. He called that \"democracy in action\" and rejected the CEO's attack narrative. The City of Staunton, VA, cancelled the contract. The city said the CEO's narrative does not reflect its values.\n\nCrossville does not need to wait for our own open feed, our own stalking case, or our own lawsuit.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nI support public safety. I do not support leasing citywide surveillance from a company that has failed on security, enabled abuse, and attacked citizens who ask questions.\n\nFirst: the cameras themselves. In late 2025, technologist Benn Jordan found Flock Condor cameras live-streaming on the open internet. No username. No password. Anyone with a link could watch live video, download about thirty days of archive, and change settings. Security researcher Jon Gaines expanded the find. 404 Media verified roughly sixty exposed cameras. Reporter Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median at Harris Road and Young Street in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself livestream while colleagues watched remotely. Jordan filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that auto-tracked him. Local TV, including Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX, confirmed exposed police camera feeds. Flock called it a \"limited misconfiguration.\" That is a product-security failure, not an activist rumor. If this vendor left live police cameras on the public internet, \"trust our cloud\" is not a policy Crossville should keep buying.\n\nSecond: misuse is not rare. It is a pattern. In Milwaukee, WI, an officer searched a woman he was dating and her ex nearly one hundred eighty times, then resigned and pleaded guilty. One of the detectives who investigated him was later charged for misusing the same system. Georgia newsrooms this summer covered a wave of firings and arrests. Savannah put six employees on leave over alleged personal searches. Closer to home, Oak Ridge's Zachary Gauthier ran personal Flock searches, pleaded guilty to official misconduct, and was sentenced. Cities also face lawsuits in Huntington, WV, Wichita, KS, and California. Taxpayers keep the downside. Flock keeps the lease check.\n\nThird: look at how this company's CEO treats the public. In December 2025, Flock CEO Garrett Langley emailed police clients that they were under a \"coordinated attack\" from activists who want to \"defund the police... and normalize lawlessness,\" and that people were \"trying to turn a public records process into a weapon.\" In a Forbes interview, he also called people who map publicly visible cameras a \"terroristic organization,\" closer to Antifa than to lawful advocacy. Staunton, VA, Police Chief Jim Williams, a forty-one year police veteran, answered him straight. He said residents raising concerns were exercising their rights. He called that \"democracy in action\" and rejected the CEO's attack narrative. The City of Staunton, VA, cancelled the contract. The city said the CEO's narrative does not reflect its values.\n\nCrossville does not need to wait for our own open feed, our own stalking case, or our own lawsuit.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 516, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_combined_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 3.7, "visuals": [{"href": "assets/flock-condor-bakersfield-koebler-livestream.png", "label": "Jason Koebler crossing a street median on open Condor livestream (Bakersfield, CA)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-benn-jordan.png", "label": "Benn Jordan on open Condor livestream (Peachtree Creek Greenway)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground-flock-ui.png", "label": "Exposed Flock admin UI (HLS stream, no password)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground.png", "label": "Open Condor feed of a playground"}, {"href": "flock.html#open-streams", "label": "Open-streams dossier (context and sources)"}]}, {"id": "combined-b", "perspective": "combined", "variant": "B", "title": "Security gaps, misuse, and a CEO who attacks oversight", "focus_note": "Koebler Bakersfield + Jordan Peachtree Creek + nationwide firings + Staunton cancel", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "I want to start off discussing the security, or lack thereof, with these Flock cameras. Technologist Benn Jordan and researcher Jon Gaines found Flock Condor people-tracking cameras streaming live with no login. 404 Media verified about sixty feeds. Reporter Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself on the open livestream. Jordan filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that tracked him. Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX reported the company confirmed exposed police camera feeds. Flock called it a limited misconfiguration. Crossville should not trust a vendor that left live feeds on the open internet.\n\nNext, we need to have a talk about accountability and transparency. Officers across the country have used Flock for personal stalking and curiosity searches, then lost jobs or faced charges. Recently in Milwaukee, WI, an officer searched a woman he was dating and her ex nearly one hundred eighty times, resigned, and pleaded guilty. An Internal Affairs detective who worked that case was later charged for personal Flock use and for secretly installing a GPS tracker. Georgia departments fired and charged officers in a summer wave of cases. Savannah put six employees on leave. Oak Ridge already proved it can happen in Tennessee. Cities from Huntington, WV, to Wichita, KS, also face lawsuits over these systems. The voters and taxpayers are going to be the ones paying out for these.\n\nLastly, we need to evaluate the leadership of Flock. CEO Garrett Langley told police clients they were under a \"coordinated attack\" from activists who want to \"normalize lawlessness,\" and that public-records requests were being turned into a \"weapon.\" In a Forbes interview, he also called people who map publicly visible cameras a \"terroristic organization,\" closer to Antifa than to lawful advocacy. Staunton, VA, Police Chief Jim Williams rejected that story. He called resident questions \"democracy in action\". Staunton cancelled their Flock subscription. The city said the CEO's narrative does not reflect its values.\n\nCrossville does not need a local scandal to act.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nI want to start off discussing the security, or lack thereof, with these Flock cameras. Technologist Benn Jordan and researcher Jon Gaines found Flock Condor people-tracking cameras streaming live with no login. 404 Media verified about sixty feeds. Reporter Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself on the open livestream. Jordan filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that tracked him. Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX reported the company confirmed exposed police camera feeds. Flock called it a limited misconfiguration. Crossville should not trust a vendor that left live feeds on the open internet.\n\nNext, we need to have a talk about accountability and transparency. Officers across the country have used Flock for personal stalking and curiosity searches, then lost jobs or faced charges. Recently in Milwaukee, WI, an officer searched a woman he was dating and her ex nearly one hundred eighty times, resigned, and pleaded guilty. An Internal Affairs detective who worked that case was later charged for personal Flock use and for secretly installing a GPS tracker. Georgia departments fired and charged officers in a summer wave of cases. Savannah put six employees on leave. Oak Ridge already proved it can happen in Tennessee. Cities from Huntington, WV, to Wichita, KS, also face lawsuits over these systems. The voters and taxpayers are going to be the ones paying out for these.\n\nLastly, we need to evaluate the leadership of Flock. CEO Garrett Langley told police clients they were under a \"coordinated attack\" from activists who want to \"normalize lawlessness,\" and that public-records requests were being turned into a \"weapon.\" In a Forbes interview, he also called people who map publicly visible cameras a \"terroristic organization,\" closer to Antifa than to lawful advocacy. Staunton, VA, Police Chief Jim Williams rejected that story. He called resident questions \"democracy in action\". Staunton cancelled their Flock subscription. The city said the CEO's narrative does not reflect its values.\n\nCrossville does not need a local scandal to act.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 423, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_combined_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 3.0, "visuals": [{"href": "assets/flock-condor-bakersfield-koebler-livestream.png", "label": "Jason Koebler crossing a street median on open Condor livestream (Bakersfield, CA)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-benn-jordan.png", "label": "Benn Jordan on open Condor livestream (Peachtree Creek Greenway)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground-flock-ui.png", "label": "Exposed Flock admin UI (HLS stream, no password)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground.png", "label": "Open Condor feed of a playground"}, {"href": "flock.html#open-streams", "label": "Open-streams dossier (context and sources)"}]}, {"id": "efficacy-a", "perspective": "efficacy", "variant": "A", "title": "Tiny hit rates for a huge privacy cost", "focus_note": "EFF and MuckRock hit rates and local cost", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "I support public safety. I also expect taxpayers to get honest numbers before we keep leasing these citywide surveillance cameras.\n\nCrossville's Flock quote shows about ten Falcon plate-reader cameras for roughly twenty-five thousand dollars a year. Atlas of Surveillance, a public camera-mapping project, listed eleven ALPRs here. That is real money for a small city, and the public still has not seen local results that show this money was well spent.\n\nWhat we do have is public-records math from other agencies. The Electronic Frontier Foundation, a digital-rights group often called EFF, published a California table showing how often scanned plates matched wanted or stolen-vehicle lists: Roseville, CA, at about 0.03 percent, Glendale, CA, at 0.04 percent, and Burbank, CA, at 0.05 percent. EFF and MuckRock, a public-records journalism project, found an average hit rate around half a percent in a wider dataset, with many agencies far lower. Piedmont, California reporting worked out to roughly 0.0013 percent, meaning millions of scans for a handful of leads. A \"hit\" on one of those lists is not a cleared case. It is often just a match to a list.\n\nVendor marketing percentages are not a substitute for Crossville publishing who searched, why, what matched, and what closed. Until those numbers are on the record, we are paying for mass scanning and taking the sales pitch on trust.\n\nIf the hit rate elsewhere is routinely well under one percent of plate scans, Crossville should not renew on slogans like \"it solves crime\" or \"extra eyes on the street.\" I am asking for a recorded answer before any money continues: what is Crossville's hit rate, what share of hits became arrests or recoveries, and how many ordinary drivers were scanned to get there? If Crossville PD and city leadership cannot put those answers in writing, the claim that this works is marketing, not budgeting.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nI support public safety. I also expect taxpayers to get honest numbers before we keep leasing these citywide surveillance cameras.\n\nCrossville's Flock quote shows about ten Falcon plate-reader cameras for roughly twenty-five thousand dollars a year. Atlas of Surveillance, a public camera-mapping project, listed eleven ALPRs here. That is real money for a small city, and the public still has not seen local results that show this money was well spent.\n\nWhat we do have is public-records math from other agencies. The Electronic Frontier Foundation, a digital-rights group often called EFF, published a California table showing how often scanned plates matched wanted or stolen-vehicle lists: Roseville, CA, at about 0.03 percent, Glendale, CA, at 0.04 percent, and Burbank, CA, at 0.05 percent. EFF and MuckRock, a public-records journalism project, found an average hit rate around half a percent in a wider dataset, with many agencies far lower. Piedmont, California reporting worked out to roughly 0.0013 percent, meaning millions of scans for a handful of leads. A \"hit\" on one of those lists is not a cleared case. It is often just a match to a list.\n\nVendor marketing percentages are not a substitute for Crossville publishing who searched, why, what matched, and what closed. Until those numbers are on the record, we are paying for mass scanning and taking the sales pitch on trust.\n\nIf the hit rate elsewhere is routinely well under one percent of plate scans, Crossville should not renew on slogans like \"it solves crime\" or \"extra eyes on the street.\" I am asking for a recorded answer before any money continues: what is Crossville's hit rate, what share of hits became arrests or recoveries, and how many ordinary drivers were scanned to get there? If Crossville PD and city leadership cannot put those answers in writing, the claim that this works is marketing, not budgeting.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 406, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_efficacy_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.9, "visuals": []}, {"id": "efficacy-b", "perspective": "efficacy", "variant": "B", "title": "Stop funding mass scanning that rarely pays off", "focus_note": "Demand a public outcome dashboard", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Please look past the sales pitch and ask what Crossville actually buys.\n\nAutomated plate readers scan ordinary drivers constantly. Independent analyses keep showing the same pattern: huge numbers of innocent scans, only a tiny fraction that match a wanted or stolen-vehicle list, and even fewer that become solved crimes. California Highway Patrol documents cited about 0.3 percent alerts per reads. Spokane, WA, and Atlanta, GA, reporting has likewise undercut claims that more cameras automatically mean more crimes get solved.\n\nCrossville has not published local hit rates. It has not published how many cases these cameras actually helped close. It has not published monthly search reports. We are told the system helps. We are not shown the numbers.\n\nMeanwhile Crossville residents pay for cameras that track where we go. If the results were strong, publishing monthly totals would be easy. The silence suggests they are not.\n\nI am not anti-police. I oppose surveillance that runs on promises instead of proof. Before any more money continues, Crossville PD and city leadership should produce a one-page public dashboard: scans, alerts, confirmed hits, cases closed with ALPR as a necessary tool, and cost per outcome. If that page cannot be produced, the city is spending money without knowing what it buys, while logging where residents drive.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nPlease look past the sales pitch and ask what Crossville actually buys.\n\nAutomated plate readers scan ordinary drivers constantly. Independent analyses keep showing the same pattern: huge numbers of innocent scans, only a tiny fraction that match a wanted or stolen-vehicle list, and even fewer that become solved crimes. California Highway Patrol documents cited about 0.3 percent alerts per reads. Spokane, WA, and Atlanta, GA, reporting has likewise undercut claims that more cameras automatically mean more crimes get solved.\n\nCrossville has not published local hit rates. It has not published how many cases these cameras actually helped close. It has not published monthly search reports. We are told the system helps. We are not shown the numbers.\n\nMeanwhile Crossville residents pay for cameras that track where we go. If the results were strong, publishing monthly totals would be easy. The silence suggests they are not.\n\nI am not anti-police. I oppose surveillance that runs on promises instead of proof. Before any more money continues, Crossville PD and city leadership should produce a one-page public dashboard: scans, alerts, confirmed hits, cases closed with ALPR as a necessary tool, and cost per outcome. If that page cannot be produced, the city is spending money without knowing what it buys, while logging where residents drive.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 302, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_efficacy_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.2, "visuals": []}, {"id": "security-a", "perspective": "security", "variant": "A", "title": "Open livestreams are not a theoretical risk", "focus_note": "Benn Jordan Peachtree Creek + Jason Koebler Bakersfield Condor exposure", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "I want to talk about security in concrete terms: what researchers and newsrooms documented.\n\nIn late 2025, technologist and independent researcher Benn Jordan showed Flock Condor cameras, the company's pan-tilt video units that track people, streaming on the open internet with no username, no password, and no encryption. He found admin interfaces through a commercial device search engine, using queries like \"Flock Admin\" on Shodan, not by hacking a locked vault. Researcher Jon Gaines, who publishes security findings as GainSec, expanded the set to dozens of live feeds and debug panels. 404 Media verified roughly sixty exposed Condors nationwide. Reporter Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median at Harris Road and Young Street in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself livestream while colleagues watched remotely. Jordan filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that auto-tracked him. The Verge summarized the same failure. Local television newsrooms, including Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX, told their viewers the company confirmed exposed live police camera feeds.\n\nThat is not a privacy activist rumor. That is a product-security failure covered by national tech press and local television news.\n\nCrossville may not run Condor models today. That is not the point. The point is whether we can trust this company. When a company's cameras can be found wide open on the public internet, \"trust our cloud\" is not a policy.\n\nHas Crossville received written notice of any exposure affecting Tennessee partners? What breach-notification and security terms are in the live contract? I am asking this body for answers on the record: which Flock models Crossville runs or plans to run, whether any Tennessee partner received exposure notices, and what breach-notification deadlines exist in the contract. A vendor that left live police cameras on the open internet has already shown it cannot be trusted with live feeds.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nI want to talk about security in concrete terms: what researchers and newsrooms documented.\n\nIn late 2025, technologist and independent researcher Benn Jordan showed Flock Condor cameras, the company's pan-tilt video units that track people, streaming on the open internet with no username, no password, and no encryption. He found admin interfaces through a commercial device search engine, using queries like \"Flock Admin\" on Shodan, not by hacking a locked vault. Researcher Jon Gaines, who publishes security findings as GainSec, expanded the set to dozens of live feeds and debug panels. 404 Media verified roughly sixty exposed Condors nationwide. Reporter Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median at Harris Road and Young Street in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself livestream while colleagues watched remotely. Jordan filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that auto-tracked him. The Verge summarized the same failure. Local television newsrooms, including Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX, told their viewers the company confirmed exposed live police camera feeds.\n\nThat is not a privacy activist rumor. That is a product-security failure covered by national tech press and local television news.\n\nCrossville may not run Condor models today. That is not the point. The point is whether we can trust this company. When a company's cameras can be found wide open on the public internet, \"trust our cloud\" is not a policy.\n\nHas Crossville received written notice of any exposure affecting Tennessee partners? What breach-notification and security terms are in the live contract? I am asking this body for answers on the record: which Flock models Crossville runs or plans to run, whether any Tennessee partner received exposure notices, and what breach-notification deadlines exist in the contract. A vendor that left live police cameras on the open internet has already shown it cannot be trusted with live feeds.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 393, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_security_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.8, "visuals": [{"href": "assets/flock-condor-bakersfield-koebler-livestream.png", "label": "Jason Koebler crossing a street median on open Condor livestream (Bakersfield, CA)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-benn-jordan.png", "label": "Benn Jordan on open Condor livestream (Peachtree Creek Greenway)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground-flock-ui.png", "label": "Exposed Flock admin UI (HLS stream, no password)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground.png", "label": "Open Condor feed of a playground"}, {"href": "flock.html#open-streams", "label": "Open-streams dossier (context and sources)"}]}, {"id": "security-b", "perspective": "security", "variant": "B", "title": "If strangers can watch the feed, cancel the lease", "focus_note": "Open livestreams: Jordan and Koebler recorded themselves on the feeds", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Please treat the Flock livestream exposure as a red flag for any renewal.\n\nBenn Jordan, a technologist and independent researcher who documented the exposure on video, compared open Condor feeds, from Flock's pan-tilt video cameras that track people, to an open video service anyone could watch: live video, days of archive, and admin panels reachable without logging in. He filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that tracked him. 404 Media's Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself on the same kind of open livestream. Security researcher Jon Gaines, who publishes as GainSec, documented dozens of exposed interfaces. Separate cybersecurity research later catalogued dozens of findings and related CVEs, public vulnerability IDs, in Flock product lines. Local stations including Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX carried the story to ordinary viewers. These were not sealed police systems. Some feeds were simply out on the internet.\n\nI do not claim Crossville's exact units were among the exposed set. I claim Crossville should not keep leasing from a vendor whose cameras were left open on the internet and covered by national tech press and local TV.\n\nPublic safety equipment that strangers can watch online is a liability and a betrayal of residents who never opted in. Please put the incident notices, the model list, and the contract security clauses on the record. Do not wait and hope the next failure skips Cumberland County. If someone with a search engine can find unprotected feeds, this city should stop paying for the product.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nPlease treat the Flock livestream exposure as a red flag for any renewal.\n\nBenn Jordan, a technologist and independent researcher who documented the exposure on video, compared open Condor feeds, from Flock's pan-tilt video cameras that track people, to an open video service anyone could watch: live video, days of archive, and admin panels reachable without logging in. He filmed himself on Peachtree Creek Greenway feeds that tracked him. 404 Media's Jason Koebler recorded himself crossing a street median in Bakersfield, CA, and watched himself on the same kind of open livestream. Security researcher Jon Gaines, who publishes as GainSec, documented dozens of exposed interfaces. Separate cybersecurity research later catalogued dozens of findings and related CVEs, public vulnerability IDs, in Flock product lines. Local stations including Denver's 9NEWS and West Palm Beach's WFLX carried the story to ordinary viewers. These were not sealed police systems. Some feeds were simply out on the internet.\n\nI do not claim Crossville's exact units were among the exposed set. I claim Crossville should not keep leasing from a vendor whose cameras were left open on the internet and covered by national tech press and local TV.\n\nPublic safety equipment that strangers can watch online is a liability and a betrayal of residents who never opted in. Please put the incident notices, the model list, and the contract security clauses on the record. Do not wait and hope the next failure skips Cumberland County. If someone with a search engine can find unprotected feeds, this city should stop paying for the product.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 344, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_security_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.5, "visuals": [{"href": "assets/flock-condor-bakersfield-koebler-livestream.png", "label": "Jason Koebler crossing a street median on open Condor livestream (Bakersfield, CA)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-benn-jordan.png", "label": "Benn Jordan on open Condor livestream (Peachtree Creek Greenway)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground-flock-ui.png", "label": "Exposed Flock admin UI (HLS stream, no password)"}, {"href": "assets/flock-condor-playground.png", "label": "Open Condor feed of a playground"}, {"href": "flock.html#open-streams", "label": "Open-streams dossier (context and sources)"}]}, {"id": "abuse-a", "perspective": "abuse", "variant": "A", "title": "Tennessee already proved misuse is real", "focus_note": "Oak Ridge, Shelby, Morristown", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Authoritarians say if you have done nothing wrong, you have nothing to hide. Tennessee's own record says officers can misuse these databases against real people.\n\nIn Oak Ridge, former officer Zachary Gauthier ran eighteen personal Flock searches, pleaded guilty to twenty-three official-misconduct counts, and was sentenced. That is not a rumor. It is a conviction covered by WBIR, WATE, WVLT, and 3B Media. Institute for Justice reporting separately describes a Shelby County ALPR misuse allegation involving more than a hundred plate lookups of an ex-wife. In Morristown, Business Insider's police-records reporting describes a Flock alert that helped trigger a gunpoint stop after a personalized plate was misread. Adults were handcuffed, and a child was left in the vehicle until the mismatch was sorted out.\n\nCrossville has not published who can query local plate reads across the East Tennessee network. When agencies share data, misuse in one city can touch drivers scanned in another.\n\nI am asking you not to shrug this off as \"elsewhere.\" Oak Ridge is in our region. Morristown is in our region. Please do not tell residents that policy alone will fix this while refusing to publish who can search. A policy with no audits is just words on paper. Oak Ridge shows what happens when personal curiosity meets a powerful lookup tool.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nAuthoritarians say if you have done nothing wrong, you have nothing to hide. Tennessee's own record says officers can misuse these databases against real people.\n\nIn Oak Ridge, former officer Zachary Gauthier ran eighteen personal Flock searches, pleaded guilty to twenty-three official-misconduct counts, and was sentenced. That is not a rumor. It is a conviction covered by WBIR, WATE, WVLT, and 3B Media. Institute for Justice reporting separately describes a Shelby County ALPR misuse allegation involving more than a hundred plate lookups of an ex-wife. In Morristown, Business Insider's police-records reporting describes a Flock alert that helped trigger a gunpoint stop after a personalized plate was misread. Adults were handcuffed, and a child was left in the vehicle until the mismatch was sorted out.\n\nCrossville has not published who can query local plate reads across the East Tennessee network. When agencies share data, misuse in one city can touch drivers scanned in another.\n\nI am asking you not to shrug this off as \"elsewhere.\" Oak Ridge is in our region. Morristown is in our region. Please do not tell residents that policy alone will fix this while refusing to publish who can search. A policy with no audits is just words on paper. Oak Ridge shows what happens when personal curiosity meets a powerful lookup tool.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 306, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_abuse_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.2, "visuals": []}, {"id": "abuse-b", "perspective": "abuse", "variant": "B", "title": "Tennessee and Georgia show a pattern, not a fluke", "focus_note": "TN + GA misconduct cluster", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Tennessee and Georgia show a pattern. This is not one isolated bad apple story.\n\nTennessee has documented felony personal use of Flock in Oak Ridge and a reported Flock-linked misread stop in Morristown. Georgia newsrooms in July 2026 covered a wave of Flock-related misconduct cases in which officers were fired or arrested after alleged personal lookups and stalking patterns. Verified misuse incidents keep growing because reporters keep finding the same tool used the same wrong way: type a plate, invent a reason, and track someone's life.\n\nThese systems make intimate stalking easier because travel history is searchable. Victims and journalists often expose the abuse, not vendor dashboards.\n\nCrossville residents deserve better than \"trust officers not to look.\" Written rules help, but they do not stop an officer who already has the search box. Georgia's July reporting wave matters because it shows a pattern of misconduct, not one bad apple. When multiple departments in the same region generate Flock abuse headlines in the same month, the product itself is part of the story: searching is easy and nothing slows it down.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nTennessee and Georgia show a pattern. This is not one isolated bad apple story.\n\nTennessee has documented felony personal use of Flock in Oak Ridge and a reported Flock-linked misread stop in Morristown. Georgia newsrooms in July 2026 covered a wave of Flock-related misconduct cases in which officers were fired or arrested after alleged personal lookups and stalking patterns. Verified misuse incidents keep growing because reporters keep finding the same tool used the same wrong way: type a plate, invent a reason, and track someone's life.\n\nThese systems make intimate stalking easier because travel history is searchable. Victims and journalists often expose the abuse, not vendor dashboards.\n\nCrossville residents deserve better than \"trust officers not to look.\" Written rules help, but they do not stop an officer who already has the search box. Georgia's July reporting wave matters because it shows a pattern of misconduct, not one bad apple. When multiple departments in the same region generate Flock abuse headlines in the same month, the product itself is part of the story: searching is easy and nothing slows it down.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 268, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_abuse_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.9, "visuals": []}, {"id": "ceo-a", "perspective": "ceo", "variant": "A", "title": "Do not renew with a vendor that smears privacy as terrorism", "focus_note": "Langley / DeFlock / Staunton, VA", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "I want to talk about the company Crossville is paying, and about what its CEO has said about people who ask questions.\n\nIn a Forbes interview, Flock CEO Garrett Langley described DeFlock, volunteers who map publicly visible camera locations, as a \"terroristic organization,\" saying they are closer to Antifa than to lawful advocacy. Mapping tax-funded cameras is not terrorism. It is transparency. Forbes later reported that he apologized for labeling activists terrorists. The quote still tells you how this vendor views public oversight.\n\nSeparately, reporting documented inaccurate public remarks about federal access. The company later walked those remarks back as poor communication. Customer emails also cast public-records requests as a \"weapon,\" and critics as people who want to \"normalize lawlessness.\" Staunton, VA, cancelled Flock after Police Chief Jim Williams called resident questions \"democracy in action\" and the city said the CEO's narrative did not reflect its values.\n\nCrossville should not keep paying a company that talks about residents that way. When this city signs with Flock, it chooses a vendor that has treated public questions as a threat. A CEO who likens transparency activists to terrorists is showing you how Crossville residents are viewed by a company with massive amounts of data about them. Do not spend local tax dollars on that.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nI want to talk about the company Crossville is paying, and about what its CEO has said about people who ask questions.\n\nIn a Forbes interview, Flock CEO Garrett Langley described DeFlock, volunteers who map publicly visible camera locations, as a \"terroristic organization,\" saying they are closer to Antifa than to lawful advocacy. Mapping tax-funded cameras is not terrorism. It is transparency. Forbes later reported that he apologized for labeling activists terrorists. The quote still tells you how this vendor views public oversight.\n\nSeparately, reporting documented inaccurate public remarks about federal access. The company later walked those remarks back as poor communication. Customer emails also cast public-records requests as a \"weapon,\" and critics as people who want to \"normalize lawlessness.\" Staunton, VA, cancelled Flock after Police Chief Jim Williams called resident questions \"democracy in action\" and the city said the CEO's narrative did not reflect its values.\n\nCrossville should not keep paying a company that talks about residents that way. When this city signs with Flock, it chooses a vendor that has treated public questions as a threat. A CEO who likens transparency activists to terrorists is showing you how Crossville residents are viewed by a company with massive amounts of data about them. Do not spend local tax dollars on that.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 300, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_ceo_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.1, "visuals": []}, {"id": "ceo-b", "perspective": "ceo", "variant": "B", "title": "Who you buy from matters", "focus_note": "FOIA hostility and terroristic comments", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Who you buy from matters, not just the camera hardware.\n\nFlock's CEO has a public record residents can read. In August 2025, the company admitted that some remarks about federal access were inaccurate. Later comments treated FOIA and public-records requests as a coordinated attack. The CEO also likened people who map publicly visible cameras to a terroristic or Antifa-style threat. That is not how a trustworthy public contractor talks about citizens.\n\nStaunton, VA, cancelled after that fight over values. Other cities have walked away over transparency fights and CEO messaging. Crossville does not need to wait for our own scandal to notice the pattern.\n\nI support the police and fully acknowledge how difficult their job is. I do not support paying a company that paints oversight as extremism. Crossville should also ask whether the vendor is truthful and accurate about federal access, respects public-records requests, and treats residents as citizens rather than enemies. On those measures, the public record is bad enough.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nWho you buy from matters, not just the camera hardware.\n\nFlock's CEO has a public record residents can read. In August 2025, the company admitted that some remarks about federal access were inaccurate. Later comments treated FOIA and public-records requests as a coordinated attack. The CEO also likened people who map publicly visible cameras to a terroristic or Antifa-style threat. That is not how a trustworthy public contractor talks about citizens.\n\nStaunton, VA, cancelled after that fight over values. Other cities have walked away over transparency fights and CEO messaging. Crossville does not need to wait for our own scandal to notice the pattern.\n\nI support the police and fully acknowledge how difficult their job is. I do not support paying a company that paints oversight as extremism. Crossville should also ask whether the vendor is truthful and accurate about federal access, respects public-records requests, and treats residents as citizens rather than enemies. On those measures, the public record is bad enough.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 251, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_ceo_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.8, "visuals": []}, {"id": "transparency-a", "perspective": "transparency", "variant": "A", "title": "\"Trust me, bro\" is not a public-records policy", "focus_note": "No map, no search policy, dead portal", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Please reject the \"trust me, bro\" model.\n\nCrossville has not published a public camera map, a written search policy residents can read, or monthly reports showing who searched what and why. The Flock \"transparency portal\" URL for Crossville has not been available to the public. Atlas of Surveillance, a public camera-mapping project, listed cameras. The city has not matched that with open documentation.\n\nIf this program were modest and carefully limited, transparency would be easy. Instead we get assurances. That is not accountability. That is asking us to approve the program with no real proof.\n\nTennessee's retention statute caps how long data may be stored. It is not a substitute for local rules, audits, or public reporting. Neighboring East Tennessee campaigns are asking the same questions the City of Crossville still has not answered on the record: who can query local reads, which partners share data, and whether FreeForm natural-language search or Multi-Geo cross-city travel tools are enabled.\n\nI am asking you to stop asking residents to take surveillance on trust. I will know transparency when I can open a city page and see the map, the rules, the partners, and last month's searches without filing a Tennessee Public Records Act request. Until then, every renewal is another vote based on trust instead of documents. That is how mass surveillance spreads.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nPlease reject the \"trust me, bro\" model.\n\nCrossville has not published a public camera map, a written search policy residents can read, or monthly reports showing who searched what and why. The Flock \"transparency portal\" URL for Crossville has not been available to the public. Atlas of Surveillance, a public camera-mapping project, listed cameras. The city has not matched that with open documentation.\n\nIf this program were modest and carefully limited, transparency would be easy. Instead we get assurances. That is not accountability. That is asking us to approve the program with no real proof.\n\nTennessee's retention statute caps how long data may be stored. It is not a substitute for local rules, audits, or public reporting. Neighboring East Tennessee campaigns are asking the same questions the City of Crossville still has not answered on the record: who can query local reads, which partners share data, and whether FreeForm natural-language search or Multi-Geo cross-city travel tools are enabled.\n\nI am asking you to stop asking residents to take surveillance on trust. I will know transparency when I can open a city page and see the map, the rules, the partners, and last month's searches without filing a Tennessee Public Records Act request. Until then, every renewal is another vote based on trust instead of documents. That is how mass surveillance spreads.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 311, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_transparency_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.2, "visuals": []}, {"id": "transparency-b", "perspective": "transparency", "variant": "B", "title": "Publish it or pull it down", "focus_note": "Monthly reports, partner list, supervisor rules", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Secrecy is not something residents voted for.\n\nA responsible program would publish a camera map, written rules on when searches are allowed, supervisor approval rules, a partner list for sharing and National Lookup enrollment, and monthly totals. Crossville has not put that package in front of the public. Calling these cameras \"extra eyes\" is not a substitute for documents.\n\nWhen cities hide the basics, the message is that oversight is optional. That is how misuse happens elsewhere, and how liability lands on taxpayers later.\n\nI am not asking for a press release. I am asking for documents the public can check. Government that says \"trust me, bro\" is how we got here. If the program is as carefully limited as supporters claim, publishing should be painless. The longer the city refuses basic disclosure, the stronger the case against keeping this system. Open records are not optional when the city logs where people drive without a warrant.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nSecrecy is not something residents voted for.\n\nA responsible program would publish a camera map, written rules on when searches are allowed, supervisor approval rules, a partner list for sharing and National Lookup enrollment, and monthly totals. Crossville has not put that package in front of the public. Calling these cameras \"extra eyes\" is not a substitute for documents.\n\nWhen cities hide the basics, the message is that oversight is optional. That is how misuse happens elsewhere, and how liability lands on taxpayers later.\n\nI am not asking for a press release. I am asking for documents the public can check. Government that says \"trust me, bro\" is how we got here. If the program is as carefully limited as supporters claim, publishing should be painless. The longer the city refuses basic disclosure, the stronger the case against keeping this system. Open records are not optional when the city logs where people drive without a warrant.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 242, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_transparency_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.7, "visuals": []}, {"id": "cancellations-a", "perspective": "cancellations", "variant": "A", "title": "Cities are giving Flock the boot. Crossville should too", "focus_note": "Dozens of cancel/suspend/non-renew votes nationwide", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "This is no longer a fringe debate. Councils keep voting these cameras out.\n\nDozens of cities and counties have cancelled, suspended, or not renewed Flock. By late July 2026, public reporting had documented about forty-nine of them. Recent votes and fights include Harrisonburg and Staunton, VA; Salem, MA; Fort Collins, CO; Monroe County, IN; Stoughton, WI; South Kingstown, RI; and packed rooms from New Jersey to Georgia demanding the same thing: get these cameras out.\n\nLocal newsrooms and national outlets have covered why communities walk away, including privacy fights, CEO messaging, and simple voter anger. Knox County paused a huge expansion after Mayor Jacobs and commissioners pushed back. Crossville does not need to be last.\n\nOther cities have already gotten rid of these cameras. Crossville should follow suit. We should not wait for a scandal here to force the issue. Those towns ended the lease and took the cameras off the streets. We can follow that path before something goes wrong locally.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nThis is no longer a fringe debate. Councils keep voting these cameras out.\n\nDozens of cities and counties have cancelled, suspended, or not renewed Flock. By late July 2026, public reporting had documented about forty-nine of them. Recent votes and fights include Harrisonburg and Staunton, VA; Salem, MA; Fort Collins, CO; Monroe County, IN; Stoughton, WI; South Kingstown, RI; and packed rooms from New Jersey to Georgia demanding the same thing: get these cameras out.\n\nLocal newsrooms and national outlets have covered why communities walk away, including privacy fights, CEO messaging, and simple voter anger. Knox County paused a huge expansion after Mayor Jacobs and commissioners pushed back. Crossville does not need to be last.\n\nOther cities have already gotten rid of these cameras. Crossville should follow suit. We should not wait for a scandal here to force the issue. Those towns ended the lease and took the cameras off the streets. We can follow that path before something goes wrong locally.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 249, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_cancellations_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.8, "visuals": []}, {"id": "cancellations-b", "perspective": "cancellations", "variant": "B", "title": "Voters are forcing cities to dump Flock", "focus_note": "Staunton, VA, democracy-in-action + NorthJersey.com / Malwarebytes backlash", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Voters are speaking up nationally, across Tennessee, and right here at home.\n\nStaunton, VA, cancelled after Police Chief Jim Williams called resident questions \"democracy in action\" and the city rejected the CEO's lawlessness narrative. NorthJersey.com reporting and national roundups describe a spreading movement to cancel contracts and remove cameras. Malwarebytes, and other publications, have summarized how and why ordinary towns are pushing back. More councils keep voting the cameras out.\n\nCrossville residents see that trend. They have watched East Tennessee pause camera expansions and Middle Tennessee debate these systems. They will notice if this body pretends nothing changed.\n\nI am asking you to join the cities that chose their voters over mass surveillance. When towns cancel these contracts, that is \"democracy in action,\" as Staunton Police Chief Jim Williams put it. Crossville's silence will be noticed. The City of Crossville should explain on the record why this town should keep what many voters want to get rid of and what so many other cities are dumping.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nVoters are speaking up nationally, across Tennessee, and right here at home.\n\nStaunton, VA, cancelled after Police Chief Jim Williams called resident questions \"democracy in action\" and the city rejected the CEO's lawlessness narrative. NorthJersey.com reporting and national roundups describe a spreading movement to cancel contracts and remove cameras. Malwarebytes, and other publications, have summarized how and why ordinary towns are pushing back. More councils keep voting the cameras out.\n\nCrossville residents see that trend. They have watched East Tennessee pause camera expansions and Middle Tennessee debate these systems. They will notice if this body pretends nothing changed.\n\nI am asking you to join the cities that chose their voters over mass surveillance. When towns cancel these contracts, that is \"democracy in action,\" as Staunton Police Chief Jim Williams put it. Crossville's silence will be noticed. The City of Crossville should explain on the record why this town should keep what many voters want to get rid of and what so many other cities are dumping.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 253, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_cancellations_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.8, "visuals": []}, {"id": "liability-a", "perspective": "liability", "variant": "A", "title": "Misreads become million-dollar taxpayer problems", "focus_note": "Gilliam $1.9M, Green $495k, Roseville 71%, Morristown caution", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Please think about liability before you renew.\n\nAutomated plate readers misread plates. When officers treat a bad read as certain, families get guns drawn on them, get handcuffed, and get traumatized, and cities write settlement checks paid for by the taxpayers and voters. Aurora, Colorado paid about 1.9 million dollars in the Gilliam case after an ALPR-related wrongful stop. San Francisco, CA, paid about 495,000 dollars in the Denise Green case. Those were not Flock-branded products. They show what ALPR errors cost taxpayers.\n\nBusiness Insider reported that Roseville, California police found Flock misread plates in about seventy-one percent of stolen or felony vehicle alerts in 2023 and 2024. Roseville said those incorrect alerts did not produce stops because officers verify first. Closer to home, Business Insider's police-records reporting describes a Morristown stop tied to a Flock alert after the system misread the letter O in a personalized plate reading LOVEY as a zero, and matched it to a stolen plate. Tennessee hasn't had to endure a multimillion-dollar settlement yet. Not getting struck by lightning yet does not mean I should play a round of golf in the middle of a storm. Tennessee's retention law in the Tennessee Code Annotated only limits how long plate data may be stored. It does not block federal civil-rights lawsuits.\n\nCrossville should not wait to be the next defendant. One bad stop can cost more than years of the camera budget. Other cities' settlements show what Crossville could pay. Roseville shows how often Flock alerts can be wrong. Morristown is the warning when verification fails. Crossville should not wait to learn the same lesson until the settlement checks are being signed.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nPlease think about liability before you renew.\n\nAutomated plate readers misread plates. When officers treat a bad read as certain, families get guns drawn on them, get handcuffed, and get traumatized, and cities write settlement checks paid for by the taxpayers and voters. Aurora, Colorado paid about 1.9 million dollars in the Gilliam case after an ALPR-related wrongful stop. San Francisco, CA, paid about 495,000 dollars in the Denise Green case. Those were not Flock-branded products. They show what ALPR errors cost taxpayers.\n\nBusiness Insider reported that Roseville, California police found Flock misread plates in about seventy-one percent of stolen or felony vehicle alerts in 2023 and 2024. Roseville said those incorrect alerts did not produce stops because officers verify first. Closer to home, Business Insider's police-records reporting describes a Morristown stop tied to a Flock alert after the system misread the letter O in a personalized plate reading LOVEY as a zero, and matched it to a stolen plate. Tennessee hasn't had to endure a multimillion-dollar settlement yet. Not getting struck by lightning yet does not mean I should play a round of golf in the middle of a storm. Tennessee's retention law in the Tennessee Code Annotated only limits how long plate data may be stored. It does not block federal civil-rights lawsuits.\n\nCrossville should not wait to be the next defendant. One bad stop can cost more than years of the camera budget. Other cities' settlements show what Crossville could pay. Roseville shows how often Flock alerts can be wrong. Morristown is the warning when verification fails. Crossville should not wait to learn the same lesson until the settlement checks are being signed.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 370, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_liability_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.6, "visuals": []}, {"id": "liability-b", "perspective": "liability", "variant": "B", "title": "The city owns the downside when the camera is wrong", "focus_note": "Wrongful-stop database + lease model", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Flock sells a camera lease. Taxpayers keep the lawsuit risk.\n\nPublic reporting keeps documenting the same pattern: plate-reader errors put innocent people at gunpoint. Settlements in other cities prove the downside is real money, often hundreds of thousands or more, spent because a machine misread a character and officers treated the alert as certain instead of slowing down to verify it.\n\nIf a bad stop turns into a lawsuit, Crossville is the defendant. Vendor marketing will not sit at the defense table. The city will.\n\nI support catching real criminals. I do not support betting taxpayer money on error-prone mass surveillance. Insurance and indemnity clauses will be waved around. They do not erase financial responsibility when a family is detained because a camera swapped a letter for a number. Removing these cameras now is cheaper than defending a lawsuit later.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nFlock sells a camera lease. Taxpayers keep the lawsuit risk.\n\nPublic reporting keeps documenting the same pattern: plate-reader errors put innocent people at gunpoint. Settlements in other cities prove the downside is real money, often hundreds of thousands or more, spent because a machine misread a character and officers treated the alert as certain instead of slowing down to verify it.\n\nIf a bad stop turns into a lawsuit, Crossville is the defendant. Vendor marketing will not sit at the defense table. The city will.\n\nI support catching real criminals. I do not support betting taxpayer money on error-prone mass surveillance. Insurance and indemnity clauses will be waved around. They do not erase financial responsibility when a family is detained because a camera swapped a letter for a number. Removing these cameras now is cheaper than defending a lawsuit later.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 228, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_liability_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.6, "visuals": []}, {"id": "sharing-a", "perspective": "sharing", "variant": "A", "title": "Warrantless travel histories and convoy profiles", "focus_note": "Multi-Geo, Convoy Search, East TN police training records", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "These cameras do more than take a picture of a plate. They feed searchable travel history.\n\nMaryvillePrivacy.org, citing East Tennessee police training records obtained through public records, describes Flock \"Multi-Geo Search\" and \"Convoy Search\", tools that track where a car went across cities and which vehicles moved together. That can sweep in families, coworkers, and church traffic with no warrant and no crime. ACLU analysis has warned about AI pattern tools that treat movement itself as suspicious. In 2024, a Norfolk, VA, circuit court ruled that when police use a dense citywide Flock camera network to pull a car's stored travel history without a warrant, that counts as a Fourth Amendment search, like tracking someone with a GPS device, and the judge blocked that evidence.\n\nCrossville has not published whether \"Multi-Geo\" or \"Convoy\" are enabled, who can run them, or which partners can query local reads. Flock's product is built for multi-agency search. That means sharing across county lines by design, unless the city blocks it in writing, and even then residents cannot verify what they cannot see.\n\nPlease answer on the record: can a Crossville plate read be searched from outside Cumberland County today? If nobody in this room can say yes or no, this city is running a system it cannot explain. Regional tracking that residents cannot check should not continue without a warrant standard.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nThese cameras do more than take a picture of a plate. They feed searchable travel history.\n\nMaryvillePrivacy.org, citing East Tennessee police training records obtained through public records, describes Flock \"Multi-Geo Search\" and \"Convoy Search\", tools that track where a car went across cities and which vehicles moved together. That can sweep in families, coworkers, and church traffic with no warrant and no crime. ACLU analysis has warned about AI pattern tools that treat movement itself as suspicious. In 2024, a Norfolk, VA, circuit court ruled that when police use a dense citywide Flock camera network to pull a car's stored travel history without a warrant, that counts as a Fourth Amendment search, like tracking someone with a GPS device, and the judge blocked that evidence.\n\nCrossville has not published whether \"Multi-Geo\" or \"Convoy\" are enabled, who can run them, or which partners can query local reads. Flock's product is built for multi-agency search. That means sharing across county lines by design, unless the city blocks it in writing, and even then residents cannot verify what they cannot see.\n\nPlease answer on the record: can a Crossville plate read be searched from outside Cumberland County today? If nobody in this room can say yes or no, this city is running a system it cannot explain. Regional tracking that residents cannot check should not continue without a warrant standard.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 317, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_sharing_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.3, "visuals": []}, {"id": "sharing-b", "perspective": "sharing", "variant": "B", "title": "Who else can search Crossville plates?", "focus_note": "Network opacity + National Lookup questions", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "If I were to ask you who has access to search a license plate read from within Crossville city limits right now, how would you answer?\n\nIf the answer is \"we're not sure\" or \"trust the network,\" that is the problem. Flock sells multi-agency visibility. National Lookup and partner sharing debates have already forced the company to walk back public claims about federal access. Crossville still has not published enrollment status, partner lists, or audit logs the public can check.\n\nConvoy-style association tools, which flag cars that travel together, turn carpools into linked files on who travels together. Multi-Geo, which tracks a plate across cities, turns everyday trips into a searchable history. If Crossville has not written a warrant rule, an officer can run those searches without ever going to a judge.\n\nI am asking you to refuse warrantless regional tracking of Cumberland County drivers. Ordinary errands should not be recorded as a registry of who travels with whom. Convoy tools do not know the difference between a suspect network and a carpool to church. This is why mass surveillance without a warrant requirement is the issue.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nIf I were to ask you who has access to search a license plate read from within Crossville city limits right now, how would you answer?\n\nIf the answer is \"we're not sure\" or \"trust the network,\" that is the problem. Flock sells multi-agency visibility. National Lookup and partner sharing debates have already forced the company to walk back public claims about federal access. Crossville still has not published enrollment status, partner lists, or audit logs the public can check.\n\nConvoy-style association tools, which flag cars that travel together, turn carpools into linked files on who travels together. Multi-Geo, which tracks a plate across cities, turns everyday trips into a searchable history. If Crossville has not written a warrant rule, an officer can run those searches without ever going to a judge.\n\nI am asking you to refuse warrantless regional tracking of Cumberland County drivers. Ordinary errands should not be recorded as a registry of who travels with whom. Convoy tools do not know the difference between a suspect network and a carpool to church. This is why mass surveillance without a warrant requirement is the issue.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 276, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_sharing_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.0, "visuals": []}, {"id": "unpopularity-a", "perspective": "unpopularity", "variant": "A", "title": "Voters are done with these cameras", "focus_note": "Packed rooms, cancel votes, Knox / Middle TN fights", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Please read the room. These cameras are unpopular with the people who elect you.\n\nAcross the country, councils that ignored packed chambers are cancelling Flock contracts under pressure. Staunton and Harrisonburg, VA; Salem, MA; Fort Collins, CO; and others show the pattern. In East Tennessee, Knox County's expansion fight is a major news topic right now. Mayor Jacobs, to his great credit, opposed hundreds of cameras. Camera installations were halted, and commissioners demanded policies. Middle Tennessee debates show Lebanon residents asking to cancel while other counties expand. That is not a quiet consensus for more surveillance.\n\nCrossville residents follow those stories. They also follow Oak Ridge misuse and Morristown misread coverage. Continuing Flock without a real public debate tells voters their privacy doesn't matter.\n\nI am asking you to side with the people who elect you, not a company whose objective is to line its own pockets. Across Tennessee and the rest of the nation, voters are speaking out against Flock and other surveillance. The cancellations in other towns and cities are the result of voters making their voices heard, and voters here in Crossville are taking notice. Bring that conversation into this chamber on the record.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nPlease read the room. These cameras are unpopular with the people who elect you.\n\nAcross the country, councils that ignored packed chambers are cancelling Flock contracts under pressure. Staunton and Harrisonburg, VA; Salem, MA; Fort Collins, CO; and others show the pattern. In East Tennessee, Knox County's expansion fight is a major news topic right now. Mayor Jacobs, to his great credit, opposed hundreds of cameras. Camera installations were halted, and commissioners demanded policies. Middle Tennessee debates show Lebanon residents asking to cancel while other counties expand. That is not a quiet consensus for more surveillance.\n\nCrossville residents follow those stories. They also follow Oak Ridge misuse and Morristown misread coverage. Continuing Flock without a real public debate tells voters their privacy doesn't matter.\n\nI am asking you to side with the people who elect you, not a company whose objective is to line its own pockets. Across Tennessee and the rest of the nation, voters are speaking out against Flock and other surveillance. The cancellations in other towns and cities are the result of voters making their voices heard, and voters here in Crossville are taking notice. Bring that conversation into this chamber on the record.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 283, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_unpopularity_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 2.0, "visuals": []}, {"id": "unpopularity-b", "perspective": "unpopularity", "variant": "B", "title": "Recorded votes beat quiet renewals", "focus_note": "Electoral accountability / cancel-backlash narrative", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "A quiet renewal is not consent. It is how unpopular surveillance programs avoid public oversight.\n\nThe national cancel wave exists because residents showed up, emailed, and made officials own a recorded vote. In Warrenton, VA, after the town council approved an Amazon data center over packed opposition, voters later replaced the members who backed that vote. That fight reminded politicians that voters remember. Flock cameras are every bit as unpopular as those data centers.\n\nCrossville should not bury this on a consent agenda. Cancel or renew should be debated in public, with residents allowed to speak, and then decided by a recorded vote.\n\nThis is how you show respect and common courtesy to the voters who elected you. Do not mistake quiet for consent. Many residents only learn these cameras exist when a scandal hits the news or a neighbor or friend points them out. Once they are aware, they are disgusted. They wonder how these cameras got approved in the first place, and they want them gone.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nA quiet renewal is not consent. It is how unpopular surveillance programs avoid public oversight.\n\nThe national cancel wave exists because residents showed up, emailed, and made officials own a recorded vote. In Warrenton, VA, after the town council approved an Amazon data center over packed opposition, voters later replaced the members who backed that vote. That fight reminded politicians that voters remember. Flock cameras are every bit as unpopular as those data centers.\n\nCrossville should not bury this on a consent agenda. Cancel or renew should be debated in public, with residents allowed to speak, and then decided by a recorded vote.\n\nThis is how you show respect and common courtesy to the voters who elected you. Do not mistake quiet for consent. Many residents only learn these cameras exist when a scandal hits the news or a neighbor or friend points them out. Once they are aware, they are disgusted. They wonder how these cameras got approved in the first place, and they want them gone.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 254, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_unpopularity_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.8, "visuals": []}, {"id": "vendor-swap-a", "perspective": "vendor-swap", "variant": "A", "title": "Swapping the logo does not restore our rights", "focus_note": "Not just Flock / Axon plate readers", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "Some will say the fix is to quit Flock and buy Axon, or another plate reader. That is not privacy.\n\nThe risk is the tool: a searchable log of where cars go, with weak rules on who can search. Cities that \"quit Flock\" and install Axon keep the same warrantless mass scanning under a new company name. Knoxville PD's Axon path does not erase the East Tennessee network problem for Crossville drivers whose plates still enter shared systems elsewhere.\n\nLeaving Flock should mean ending the surveillance tool, not quietly rebranding the same mass surveillance under another logo.\n\nDo not trade one logo for another and call it reform. If a future agenda swaps Flock for Axon without a real public debate, that is the same problem with a new company name. The risk is the tool, not the logo stamped on the device.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nSome will say the fix is to quit Flock and buy Axon, or another plate reader. That is not privacy.\n\nThe risk is the tool: a searchable log of where cars go, with weak rules on who can search. Cities that \"quit Flock\" and install Axon keep the same warrantless mass scanning under a new company name. Knoxville PD's Axon path does not erase the East Tennessee network problem for Crossville drivers whose plates still enter shared systems elsewhere.\n\nLeaving Flock should mean ending the surveillance tool, not quietly rebranding the same mass surveillance under another logo.\n\nDo not trade one logo for another and call it reform. If a future agenda swaps Flock for Axon without a real public debate, that is the same problem with a new company name. The risk is the tool, not the logo stamped on the device.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 229, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_vendor-swap_a.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.6, "visuals": []}, {"id": "vendor-swap-b", "perspective": "vendor-swap", "variant": "B", "title": "Ban mass scanning, not just the brand name", "focus_note": "Surveillance equipment vote rule for any vendor", "opening": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.", "body": "If Crossville cancels Flock but leaves the door open for the next plate-reader company, nothing real has changed.\n\nFlock is today's vendor. Tomorrow it could be Axon Fleet, Vigilant, or another ALPR cloud. The problem is mass location tracking without a warrant. That is a Fourth Amendment issue, not a question of which company prints the brochure.\n\nKnoxville PD's Axon path shows how easy it is to swap logos and keep the same kind of searchable travel history. Crossville drivers still get caught in East Tennessee networks when plates feed shared systems elsewhere.\n\nProtect Crossville from this kind of surveillance, not just one brand having a bad week. Plate readers, people-search features, and AI pattern tools raise the same problem under any logo.", "ask": "My ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "full_text": "Mayor Crawford, members of City Council. My name is [Your Name], and I live in Crossville.\n\nIf Crossville cancels Flock but leaves the door open for the next plate-reader company, nothing real has changed.\n\nFlock is today's vendor. Tomorrow it could be Axon Fleet, Vigilant, or another ALPR cloud. The problem is mass location tracking without a warrant. That is a Fourth Amendment issue, not a question of which company prints the brochure.\n\nKnoxville PD's Axon path shows how easy it is to swap logos and keep the same kind of searchable travel history. Crossville drivers still get caught in East Tennessee networks when plates feed shared systems elsewhere.\n\nProtect Crossville from this kind of surveillance, not just one brand having a bad week. Plate readers, people-search features, and AI pattern tools raise the same problem under any logo.\n\nMy ask is clear. Cancel Crossville's Flock contract and remove the cameras. Require a public hearing and a recorded City Council vote before any surveillance equipment is ever proposed again. If you will not cancel yet, take a recorded yes-or-no vote each year before any renewal, with no automatic renewal that skips a public vote, and publish monthly use reports the public can check. Thank you. I yield.", "word_count": 211, "pdf": "docs/speeches/Council_Speech_vendor-swap_b.pdf", "approx_minutes": 1.5, "visuals": []}]};
